<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.16.2: What Is an Arithmetic Sequence?</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01151</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.16.2: What Is an Arithmetic Sequence?</md:title>
    <md:uuid>32aa66ab-354b-47dd-b0f5-d3c5e7a8f3b4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Here are the values of the first 5 terms of 3 sequences:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>
    30, 40, 50, 60, 70, . . .
 </item>
  <item>
    0, 5, 15, 30, 50, . . .
 </item>
  <item>
    1, 2, 4, 8, 16, . . .
 </item>
</list>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        For each sequence, describe a way to produce a new term from the previous term.

        <para>
            30, 40, 50, 60, 70, . . .
        </para>
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Each term is 10 more than the previous term.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
          0, 5, 15, 30, 50, . . .
      </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Each term is the result of adding 5 more than was added to the previous term (add 5, then add 10, then add 15).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
          1, 2, 4, 8, 16, . . .
      </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Each term is double the previous term.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        If the patterns you described continue, which sequence has the second-greatest value for the 10th term?


    </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Sequence B</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Which of these could be geometric sequences? Explain how you know.


    </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Sequence C. For example: A geometric sequence has the same multiplier from one term to the next, the common ratio, and neither A nor B has this. Sequence C does; the common ratio is 2.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>An <term class="glossary-tooltip">arithmetic sequence</term> is a sequence in which each term is the previous term plus a constant.
  This constant is called the <term class="glossary-tooltip">common difference</term>.
</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Which of these could be arithmetic sequences? Explain how you know.


    </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Sequence A. For example: An arithmetic sequence gets each term by adding the same constant to the previous term. Sequence A adds 10 to each term to find the next term.
  </para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<title> Video: Comparing Sequences</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about comparing arithmetic and geometric sequences.
</para>
